<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bissau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bissau is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a>, on the estuary of the Geba river. The Portuguese founded a fortified trading post there in 1687, and the Fortaleza de São José da Amura was built in the 18th century. It replaced Bolama as the colonial capital in 1941, and in 1959 a dockworkers' strike at Pidjiguiti was violently suppressed, turning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paigc">PAIGC</a> towards armed struggle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bissau.jpg" alt="Bissau" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Colleen Taugher from Lewiston Idaho, USA; Modifications: The original uploader was Cornischong at Luxembourgish Wikipedia. · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bissau-Stroossenzeen--w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1588 the town of Cacheu was founded as a fortified trading base, followed by <b>Bissau</b> in the late 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-guinea">Portuguese Guinea</a></li></ul></section>
